NAME "How many states the shift+tab cycle really has (answered: four — see 49)"
WINDOW 100 34
TARGET both
SETTINGS '{"agentMode":"plan","showTips":false}'

# ANSWERED: four. official-docs/cli/modes.md gives the lap as
# default > accept-edits > auto > plan > default, so `default` is its own
# stop and not a synonym for `auto`. 49_mode_cycle_order asserts that lap as
# a table; this scene is kept as the experiment that framed the question, and
# photographs four presses for the record.
#
# Why the count alone could not settle it: on a host whose terminal sandbox is
# unavailable the binary declines auto ("Auto mode is not available: it needs
# the OS terminal sandbox...") and shows default's screen for both, so the
# cycle here is the three-stop one the documentation describes for that case.
# A count taken on this host measures the degraded cycle, not the shipped one.
#
# The question it was written to answer: is `default` a stop in the cycle, or
# is it the same state as `auto`?
#
#     M1  four states   default -> accept-edits -> auto -> plan -> default
#     M2  three states  default/auto -> accept-edits -> plan -> default/auto
#
# The status row settles the count. The composer row is photographed next to
# it for a different reason: auto names itself there ("Auto mode: edits &
# safe commands auto-approved (shift+tab to cycle)" is in agy's string
# table), so a bare ">" means the binary is in default, not in auto.

BOOT_BOTH
WAIT_READY 15s

SNAPSHOT "01_boot_plan"

KEYS "BTab"
SLEEP 900ms
SNAPSHOT "02_after_one"

KEYS "BTab"
SLEEP 900ms
SNAPSHOT "03_after_two"

KEYS "BTab"
SLEEP 900ms
SNAPSHOT "04_after_three"

DIFF
CLEANUP
